.TH LABEL 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
label \- show or set a device's label
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B label
.I device
.RI [ label ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With only a
.IR device ,
.B label
prints its label (nothing when it has none). With a
.IR label ,
it sets it; an empty one,
.BR \(dq\(dq ,
clears it.
.PP
A
.I device
is named as
.BR lsdev (1)
names it
.RB ( storage0 ,
.BR eeprom ),
with or without
.B /dev/
before it, or by its id.
.PP
The label is kept on the device's item, and shown in its details (hold
Shift over it), so it goes wherever the device goes.
.B lsdev
shows every device's label.
A label is at most 32 printable characters.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the label was printed or set; 1 for a device that isn't there, a
label that can't be one, or a device that can't have one (a cabled block's:
it has no item to keep it on).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
label storage1 backups
label eeprom
label storage1 ""
.fi
.SH NOTES
Linux labels a disk's filesystem
.RB ( e2label ,
.BR fatlabel );
mcos labels any device that is an item, as OpenOS did.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsdev (1)
